Based on my analysis, this panoramic radiograph shows extensive dental restorations (crowns, bridges, posts) and mostly intact periapical bone with a few subtle, ill‑defined periapical radiolucencies.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.585, 0.612, 0.663, 0.704], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.511, 0.619, 0.550, 0.689], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.421, 0.654, 0.495, 0.743], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Note on confidence: I examined the image at multiple contrast/zoom levels. The periapical findings I could support are all diffuse bone-structure changes without sharply defined radiolucent cavities (hence PAI 3): the periapical bone of the lower‑left (image‑right) premolar/molar region and the lower anterior incisor region. I deliberately excluded the discrete round lucencies seen mid‑root in the upper‑right premolars — they appear bilaterally symmetrical and lie in the coronal root, consistent with normal pulp chambers rather than periapical lesions. If you have ground‑truth annotations, I'd be glad to compare and recalibrate.
